  {
   "id": "crush-operators-md-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "# Operators\n\nCrush's operators are deliberately few. Everything in this chapter is verified\nagainst the current compiler.\n\n## Arithmetic\n\n| Operator | Meaning | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `+` | add / string concatenation | `5 + 3`, `\"a\" + \"b\"` |\n| `-` | subtract / unary negate | `10 - 4`, `-x` |\n| `*` | multiply | `6 * 7` |\n| `/` | divide (`Int / Int` truncates toward zero) | `20 / 4`, `-7 / 2` |\n| `%` | remainder | `17 % 5` |\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-operators-1",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "print(5 + 3)\nprint(10 - 4)\nprint(6 * 7)\nprint(20 / 4)\nprint(17 % 5)\nprint(-7 / 2)\nprint(-(3 + 2))\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "runnable"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/operators.md:16",
      "browser": "run",
      "expect_output": "8\n6\n42\n5\n2\n-3\n-5\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-operators-md-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "Mixing `Int` and `Float` promotes to `Float` (`1 + 2.5` is `3.5`). `+` between a\nstring and a number concatenates (`\"n=\" + 5` is `\"n=5\"`); `*` on a string does\n**not** repeat it.\n\nThere is no exponent operator (`**`), floor-division (`//`), or compound\nassignment (`+=`, `-=`, …) — use `math.pow(a, b)` (with `--stdlib`) and\n`x = x + 1`. See [Variables](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/variables.html).\n\n## Comparison\n\n`==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=` return `Bool`. Operands must be of compatible\ntypes; comparing a string with an int is a compile error. Strings compare\nlexicographically; arrays compare element-wise with `==`.\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-operators-2",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "let a = 10\nlet b = 20\nprint(a == b)\nprint(a != b)\nprint(a < b)\nprint(a >= b)\nprint(\"apple\" < \"banana\")\nprint([1, 2] == [1, 2])\nprint(1 == 1.0)\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "runnable"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/operators.md:51",
      "browser": "run",
      "expect_output": "false\ntrue\ntrue\nfalse\ntrue\ntrue\ntrue\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-operators-md-5",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "## Logical\n\nLogic uses **symbols**: `&&`, `||`, `!`. The words `and`, `or` and `not` are not\noperators. `&&` and `||` short-circuit (crush-ast **CRUSH-125**):\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "done"
   },
   "meta": {},
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-operators-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fn noisy() {\n    print(\"evaluated\")\n    return true\n}\n\nprint(false && noisy())\nprint(true || noisy())\nprint(!(1 > 2))\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "runnable"
    ],
    "extra": {
     "guide": {
      "src": "crush/operators.md:79",
      "browser": "run",
      "expect_output": "false\ntrue\ntrue\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "crush-operators-4",
   "kind": {
    "type": "crush"
   },
   "source": "fn main() {\n    print(true and false)\n}\n",
   "state": {
    "status": "pending"
   },
   "meta": {
    "tags": [
     "guide",
     "expected-failure"
    ],
